1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,750
The Nutmeg Estates That
Became Orchard Road

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:06,307
Orchard Road today is glass towers,

3
00:00:06,387 --> 00:00:10,527
luxury malls and a mile of
shop windows, one of the most

4
00:00:10,607 --> 00:00:14,375
expensive stretches of
retail real estate in Asia.

5
00:00:14,375 --> 00:00:18,466
Its name is a leftover clue
to something else entirely:

6
00:00:18,546 --> 00:00:23,698
in the 1830s and 40s this same ground
was thick with actual orchards,

7
00:00:23,778 --> 00:00:26,049
planted by colonial speculators

8
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:30,375
chasing a spice fortune that
lasted barely a generation.

9
00:00:30,375 --> 00:00:33,389
Nutmeg was the biggest
bet of all, and while the

10
00:00:33,469 --> 00:00:36,805
plantations themselves have
vanished without a trace,

11
00:00:36,885 --> 00:00:40,414
the men who planted them are
still on the street signs —

12
00:00:40,494 --> 00:00:43,975
Oxley, Cairnhill, Cuppage,
Claymore, Scotts, Killiney.

13
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:47,287
Singapore's most expensive
shopping street is,

14
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:51,275
underneath its signage, a
map of a spice that failed.

15
00:00:51,275 --> 00:00:56,367
For centuries, nutmeg had been one of
the most valuable commodities on earth,

16
00:00:56,447 --> 00:01:00,196
grown almost nowhere else on
the planet outside a handful

17
00:01:00,276 --> 00:01:03,756
of small islands in the
Dutch East Indies' Banda Sea,

18
00:01:03,836 --> 00:01:09,075
where the Dutch had spent two hundred
years enforcing a brutal monopoly on it.

19
00:01:09,075 --> 00:01:13,057
Breaking that monopoly was one
of Stamford Raffles's ambitions

20
00:01:13,137 --> 00:01:16,595
from the moment he founded
Singapore: within months of

21
00:01:16,675 --> 00:01:20,198
his second visit in 1819, he
had nutmeg seeds and trees

22
00:01:20,278 --> 00:01:24,261
shipped in from the East India
Company's outpost at Bencoolen,

23
00:01:24,341 --> 00:01:27,995
on Sumatra, and planted as an
experiment on the slopes of

24
00:01:28,075 --> 00:01:31,875
what was then still called
Government Hill — Fort Canning.

25
00:01:31,875 --> 00:01:36,271
The experiment itself was
modest, but it set a pattern that

26
00:01:36,351 --> 00:01:40,671
Singapore's merchants and
colonial officials followed with

27
00:01:40,751 --> 00:01:44,388
real enthusiasm through
the late 1830s and 1840s,

28
00:01:44,468 --> 00:01:49,475
when existing nutmeg plantations
were returning very good profits.

29
00:01:49,475 --> 00:01:54,210
It became, in the words of one contemporary
account, an "unthinking mania":

30
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:57,549
planters carved country
estates out of the low hills

31
00:01:57,629 --> 00:02:01,787
just inland from the town — around
what is now Tanglin, Cairnhill,

32
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:05,511
Claymore and Emerald Hill —
and the hills were fashionably

33
00:02:05,591 --> 00:02:09,700
renamed after whoever owned the
biggest bungalow on top of them.

34
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:13,750
For a couple of decades it
looked like a very good bet.

35
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:16,762
Land was cheap, prices
were high, and a nutmeg

36
00:02:16,842 --> 00:02:19,652
estate promised the
kind of return that had

37
00:02:19,732 --> 00:02:23,025
already made fortunes for
the Dutch further east.

38
00:02:23,025 --> 00:02:26,033
Merchants, surgeons and harbour officials

39
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:28,750
alike put their savings into trees.

40
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:31,108
Singapore's street map still carries

41
00:02:31,188 --> 00:02:33,625
the names of the men who planted it.

42
00:02:33,625 --> 00:02:39,097
William Cuppage, a postal clerk who
rose to become acting Postmaster

43
00:02:39,177 --> 00:02:43,669
General in the 1840s, leased
land on what became Emerald

44
00:02:43,749 --> 00:02:49,711
Hill from 1837 and secured the freehold
for his nutmeg plantation in 1845;

45
00:02:49,791 --> 00:02:54,200
his name survives in Cuppage
Road and Cuppage Terrace.

46
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:58,810
Dr Thomas Oxley, a colonial surgeon,
bought some 173 acres bounded

47
00:02:58,890 --> 00:03:02,718
by what are now Orchard,
Grange and River Valley Roads,

48
00:03:02,798 --> 00:03:08,616
planted it with nutmeg as Killiney Estate,
and built a house on top of it in 1842 —

49
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:12,595
commemorated today in both
Oxley Road and Killiney Road,

50
00:03:12,675 --> 00:03:18,075
with Grange Road recalling a second
house, Grange House, on the same estate.

51
00:03:18,075 --> 00:03:21,988
A little further along,
Charles Carnie's house,

52
00:03:22,068 --> 00:03:26,575
built around 1840 on what
was then called Carnie Hill,

53
00:03:26,655 --> 00:03:32,437
gave the area its modern name once
the spelling drifted to Cairnhill;

54
00:03:32,517 --> 00:03:36,850
by 1848 his 68-acre estate
held 4,370 nutmeg trees.

55
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:41,552
Captain William Scott, Singapore's
Harbour Master and Postmaster,

56
00:03:41,632 --> 00:03:45,525
ran an even bigger operation
nearby, Claymore Estate —

57
00:03:45,605 --> 00:03:52,146
the third-largest nutmeg plantation in Singapore,
with about 5,200 trees by the same year,

58
00:03:52,226 --> 00:03:56,125
remembered now in both
Claymore Road and Scotts Road.

59
00:03:56,125 --> 00:04:00,073
None of these men left behind
a portrait that has survived,

60
00:04:00,153 --> 00:04:03,349
and little personal detail
about most of them is

61
00:04:03,429 --> 00:04:07,525
recorded beyond the plantation
ledgers and the street signs.

62
00:04:07,525 --> 00:04:10,978
What is left is simpler,
and in its way more telling:

63
00:04:11,058 --> 00:04:14,845
an entire grid of roads in the
middle of modern Singapore,

64
00:04:14,925 --> 00:04:19,725
still named for a crop that has not
grown there in a century and a half.

65
00:04:19,725 --> 00:04:24,450
The nutmeg mania collapsed
almost as fast as it had begun.

66
00:04:24,450 --> 00:04:30,174
Sometime in the 1840s a disease the
planters called "nutmeg canker" appeared,

67
00:04:30,254 --> 00:04:34,400
causing the fruit to wither
and drop before it ripened.

68
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:38,883
Nobody at the time understood
the cause: an 1865 writer blamed

69
00:04:38,963 --> 00:04:42,931
careless cultivation by
inexperienced amateur planters,

70
00:04:43,011 --> 00:04:47,861
others suspected an insect, and it
would be more than three decades

71
00:04:47,941 --> 00:04:52,725
before the botanist H. N. Ridley
confirmed a beetle was to blame.

72
00:04:52,725 --> 00:04:56,075
Whatever the cause, the effect was total.

73
00:04:56,075 --> 00:05:00,327
The blight spread from estate to
estate through the 1850s until,

74
00:05:00,407 --> 00:05:03,847
as one account from the
time put it, there was "ruin

75
00:05:03,927 --> 00:05:07,650
staring the proprietors of
the plantation in the face."

76
00:05:07,650 --> 00:05:11,595
By 1862, commercial nutmeg
cultivation in Singapore

77
00:05:11,675 --> 00:05:14,989
had effectively ceased,
and the big estates

78
00:05:15,069 --> 00:05:18,700
were broken up into
smaller lots and sold off.

79
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:23,850
The scale of the wipeout is easy
to underestimate at this distance.

80
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:28,780
Even Singapore's own Botanic
Gardens began on the wreckage of it:

81
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:32,403
when the Agri-Horticultural
Society acquired 23

82
00:05:32,483 --> 00:05:37,491
hectares of land at Tanglin in
November 1859 to found the gardens,

83
00:05:37,571 --> 00:05:41,425
what it bought was an
abandoned nutmeg plantation.

84
00:05:41,425 --> 00:05:45,681
The Scottish planter Lawrence
Niven was hired the following year

85
00:05:45,761 --> 00:05:49,610
to develop the site, and the
bungalow built for him there,

86
00:05:49,690 --> 00:05:54,352
completed in 1868 and known today
as Burkill Hall, is described on its

87
00:05:54,432 --> 00:05:58,417
own heritage marker as the last
Anglo-Malay plantation house

88
00:05:58,497 --> 00:06:02,900
left in Singapore — built on ground
that had been nutmeg orchard.

89
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:07,076
With the nutmeg gone, the big
estates were subdivided and passed

90
00:06:07,156 --> 00:06:11,532
from owner to owner, and through
the second half of the century the

91
00:06:11,612 --> 00:06:15,855
area filled in as a quiet suburb
of bungalows and private houses,

92
00:06:15,935 --> 00:06:20,325
still known by the planters' old
names but no longer agricultural.

93
00:06:20,325 --> 00:06:24,431
Cuppage himself moved onto
his Emerald Hill land in

94
00:06:24,511 --> 00:06:27,714
the early 1850s, built two houses there,

95
00:06:27,794 --> 00:06:32,800
and lived out his life on the
estate until his death in 1872.

96
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:37,036
It then passed to his daughters,
and in 1890 to a son-in-law,

97
00:06:37,116 --> 00:06:40,785
the lawyer Edwin Koek, whose
own attempt to run it as

98
00:06:40,865 --> 00:06:45,384
an orchard also failed; the property
changed hands again in 1891,

99
00:06:45,464 --> 00:06:50,690
and by the turn of the century belonged
to two Straits Chinese businessmen,

100
00:06:50,770 --> 00:06:53,175
Seah Boon Kang and Seah Boon Kiat.

101
00:06:53,175 --> 00:06:57,045
In 1901 the Seah brothers
subdivided the estate into

102
00:06:57,125 --> 00:07:00,919
38 building lots, keeping
ten and selling the rest;

103
00:07:00,999 --> 00:07:05,021
among the buyers was the
prominent Peranakan physician

104
00:07:05,101 --> 00:07:08,975
and community leader Lim
Boon Keng, who took three.

105
00:07:08,975 --> 00:07:13,955
The first house went up in 1902,
roughly a third of the street was

106
00:07:14,035 --> 00:07:20,089
built by 1918, and by the 1930s a clear
majority of Emerald Hill Road's houses —

107
00:07:20,169 --> 00:07:25,073
at least 59 of 112 — were owned
by Peranakan, or Straits Chinese,

108
00:07:25,153 --> 00:07:29,600
families, drawn by a fashionable
new street close to town.

109
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:33,417
The result, largely designed
by the architect R. T. Rajoo,

110
00:07:33,497 --> 00:07:38,187
is one of Singapore's best-preserved
runs of Peranakan terrace housing:

111
00:07:38,267 --> 00:07:42,486
two- and three-storey homes that
pair Georgian shuttered windows

112
00:07:42,566 --> 00:07:47,928
and columns with brightly coloured ceramic
tiles and Chinese Baroque plasterwork,

113
00:07:48,008 --> 00:07:51,300
a style now simply called
Peranakan architecture.

114
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:54,369
A short walk from Emerald
Hill, on Oxley Road —

115
00:07:54,449 --> 00:07:59,595
Dr Oxley's old Killiney Estate — is where
Singapore's founding prime minister,

116
00:07:59,675 --> 00:08:02,409
Lee Kuan Yew, made his home, and his wife,

117
00:08:02,489 --> 00:08:05,893
Kwa Geok Choo, came from a
Peranakan family herself,

118
00:08:05,973 --> 00:08:09,725
very much in keeping with the
neighbourhood's character.

119
00:08:09,725 --> 00:08:13,324
It is a small, pleasant
coincidence of geography rather

120
00:08:13,404 --> 00:08:16,669
than anything more: the
street where the country's

121
00:08:16,749 --> 00:08:20,750
first leader lived carries the
name of the surgeon who tried,

122
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:24,175
and failed, to grow nutmeg
there a century before.

123
00:08:24,175 --> 00:08:28,197
Well into the 20th century the
stretch now known as Orchard

124
00:08:28,277 --> 00:08:31,255
Road stayed largely
residential — bungalows,

125
00:08:31,335 --> 00:08:35,843
mission schools, a scattering of
small shops, a quiet lane a short

126
00:08:35,923 --> 00:08:40,025
ride from the commercial docks
and offices of the old town.

127
00:08:40,025 --> 00:08:44,083
Its turn into a shopping district
came later, and gradually:

128
00:08:44,163 --> 00:08:48,083
department stores and cinemas
began opening along the road

129
00:08:48,163 --> 00:08:52,910
from the 1950s and 60s, and the pace
picked up sharply from the 1970s,

130
00:08:52,990 --> 00:08:56,841
as land was cleared and rebuilt
for the shopping centres,

131
00:08:56,921 --> 00:08:59,875
hotels and office towers
aimed at a growing,

132
00:08:59,955 --> 00:09:04,300
increasingly affluent population
and a rising tide of tourists.

133
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:08,677
By the 1980s, Orchard Road had
become what it still is today —

134
00:09:08,757 --> 00:09:12,991
Singapore's premier shopping
street — with almost nothing of

135
00:09:13,071 --> 00:09:17,600
its 19th-century suburban character
left standing above ground.

136
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:20,350
Almost nothing, but not quite.

137
00:09:20,350 --> 00:09:25,114
Outside ION Orchard, at the very
centre of the modern shopping belt,

138
00:09:25,194 --> 00:09:29,816
stands a two-tonne bronze sculpture
by the artist Kumari Nahappan:

139
00:09:29,896 --> 00:09:33,093
an opened nutmeg seed
nearly five metres long,

140
00:09:33,173 --> 00:09:37,296
its crimson mace unfurled,
unveiled in 2009 as a deliberate

141
00:09:37,376 --> 00:09:41,650
nod to the plantations that
once stood on that exact ground.

142
00:09:41,650 --> 00:09:46,600
It is easy to walk past without
noticing what it is a picture of.

143
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:50,492
One actual nutmeg tree still
stands in Singapore today,

144
00:09:50,572 --> 00:09:54,609
a few kilometres from Orchard
Road: a specimen behind the

145
00:09:54,689 --> 00:09:58,148
Garage Building at the
Singapore Botanic Gardens,

146
00:09:58,228 --> 00:10:02,698
protected under the National
Parks Board's Heritage Tree scheme

147
00:10:02,778 --> 00:10:06,750
as a living link to Raffles's
original 1819 experiment.

148
00:10:06,750 --> 00:10:09,621
It is the exception that proves the rule —

149
00:10:09,701 --> 00:10:12,782
one tree, carefully
conserved as a curiosity,

150
00:10:12,862 --> 00:10:16,375
on ground where thousands
once grew as a business.

151
00:10:16,375 --> 00:10:20,637
Why it matters today: Orchard
Road's route from failed cash crop,

152
00:10:20,717 --> 00:10:24,378
through a Peranakan suburb,
to global shopping street is

153
00:10:24,458 --> 00:10:28,253
a reminder that the ground
under Singapore's most familiar

154
00:10:28,333 --> 00:10:32,462
places has usually been something
else first, and that a street

155
00:10:32,542 --> 00:10:36,550
name can be the only monument
a vanished industry ever gets.

156
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:39,804
Nutmeg itself never
disappeared from the region —

157
00:10:39,884 --> 00:10:44,159
head up the Malay Peninsula to
Penang today, and the trees still

158
00:10:44,239 --> 00:10:48,446
bear fruit around Balik Pulau,
sold everywhere as dried nutmeg,

159
00:10:48,526 --> 00:10:51,780
pickles, juice, balm and
oil, a living trade that

160
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:55,875
Singapore's own plantations
never got the chance to become.
